/* AI Agents and Agentic Systems — 45 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '05',
  slug: 'agents',
  title: 'AI Agents & Agentic Systems',
  blurb: 'Agent loops, tool design, memory, orchestration, and the safety boundaries that keep autonomy from becoming an incident.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'What is an AI agent, and how does it differ from a simple LLM call?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `An agent is an LLM placed in a loop with tools and state, where <em>the model decides the control flow</em>. A simple call is one input to one output; an agent observes, chooses an action, executes it, observes the result, and repeats until it judges the task complete.<br><br>The three additions are <strong>tools</strong> (the ability to act on the world), <strong>memory/state</strong> (carrying information across steps), and <strong>autonomy over iteration</strong> (the model, not your code, decides what happens next and when to stop).<br><br><strong>The distinction that matters in practice:</strong> a fixed chain where you wrote the sequence is a workflow, not an agent — even if it has five LLM calls and tool use. The dividing line is whether the sequence is determined at runtime by the model. That is worth stating because <em>workflows are usually the better engineering choice</em>: they are cheaper, faster, testable, and debuggable. Agents earn their cost only when the task genuinely cannot be sequenced in advance.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> handing control flow to a probabilistic system means unbounded cost, non-deterministic failure, and a much larger security surface — which is why production agents need step budgets, spend caps, and a tool layer that enforces permissions rather than trusting the model.` },

{ q: 'Explain AI agent memory.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Agent memory is everything the agent carries beyond the current prompt, and it exists because the context window is finite while tasks and conversations are not.<br><br><strong>The working set:</strong> <em>short-term / working memory</em> is the active context — recent steps, tool outputs, the current plan. <em>Long-term memory</em> is external storage (vector index, key-value store, database) retrieved on demand. <em>Episodic memory</em> records what happened in past sessions — actions taken, outcomes, corrections. <em>Semantic memory</em> holds distilled facts, and <em>procedural memory</em> holds learned how-to knowledge, often just accumulated instructions.<br><br><strong>The production architecture that actually works</strong> is hybrid: recent steps verbatim, a rolling summary of older ones, and a <strong>structured store of durable facts</strong> (user preferences, constraints, decisions) injected unconditionally. That third component is the one teams skip and the one that matters — "the user is on the enterprise plan" must survive step 200, and recursive summarization erodes exactly these specifics.<br><br><strong>The failure mode to design against:</strong> summarizing summaries compounds error — each pass re-encodes a lossy encoding, so facts drift and then become confidently wrong. Summarize from originals where possible and keep extracted facts verbatim rather than paraphrased.<br><br><strong>And test it:</strong> plant a fact early in a long trajectory and query it much later. Single-step evals cannot detect memory failures, which is where most agents actually break.` },

{ q: 'What is harness engineering in AI?', tags: [], a: `The harness is everything wrapped around the model that turns raw next-token prediction into a working system: the tool definitions and their schemas, the observation formatting, the loop control, error handling, retries, budgets, state management, and the safety boundary.<br><br><strong>Why it is named as a discipline:</strong> with a fixed frontier model, the harness is the main variable you control, and its design changes task completion rates dramatically. The same model with well-specified tools, clean observations, and sensible retry semantics will complete tasks that it fails at with a poorly designed harness — the difference is often larger than a model upgrade.<br><br><strong>The high-leverage decisions:</strong> <em>action-space design</em> — a few well-scoped tools beat thirty overlapping ones, because tool selection accuracy degrades with count and overlap; <em>observation formatting</em> — tool results must be truncated, structured, and informative, since dumping a 50k-token API response destroys the context that the reasoning depends on; <em>error surfaces</em> — an error message that says what went wrong and what to try next lets the model self-correct, while a bare stack trace does not; and <em>loop control</em> — step budgets, timeouts, and termination conditions.<br><br><strong>The framing worth stating:</strong> most "the model isn't smart enough" conclusions are harness failures. Before switching models, read the trajectory and check whether the model had the information and affordances it needed to succeed.` },

{ q: 'Explain the ReAct agent architecture.', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `ReAct interleaves <strong>Reasoning</strong> and <strong>Acting</strong>: Thought → Action → Observation → Thought → … → Answer. The model reasons about what it needs, emits a tool call, receives the real result as an observation, and reasons again with that grounded information in context.<br><br>It fixes the complementary weaknesses of the two prior approaches. Pure chain-of-thought reasons in a closed world and hallucinates facts with no way to verify them. Action-only agents call tools without planning and flail. ReAct grounds reasoning in external observations while keeping the reasoning that decides which action makes sense.<br><br>Modern native tool/function calling is essentially ReAct with the action step moved from parsed text into a structured API — far more reliable than regex-parsing <code>Action: search[...]</code> out of free text, which was the original implementation and a constant source of failures.<br><br><strong>The failure modes to name:</strong> loops, where the model repeats a failing call because nothing in its trace signals to stop — mitigate with step budgets, call deduplication, and injecting repetition history so the model can <em>see</em> it is repeating; context growth, since every observation accumulates, requiring truncation or summarization of tool outputs; and error propagation, where a bad observation is treated as ground truth.<br><br>Production ReAct needs a hard step limit, per-step timeouts, and a defined fallback when the budget is exhausted — otherwise one malformed tool response burns an unbounded number of tokens.` },

{ q: 'What is the Plan-and-Execute agent pattern?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Plan-and-Execute separates planning from execution: a planner LLM decomposes the task into an explicit ordered list of steps up front, then a cheaper executor carries out each step, with a replanner invoked when a step fails or reality diverges from the plan.<br><br><strong>Advantages over ReAct:</strong> the plan is visible before anything happens, so it can be inspected, approved, or edited by a human — a genuine safety property. Execution can use a smaller model, cutting cost substantially. Independent steps can run in parallel. And the agent is less likely to wander, because the objective is fixed rather than re-derived at each step.<br><br><strong>The weakness is rigidity.</strong> The planner commits before it has seen any observations, so plans built on wrong assumptions fail — which is why replanning is not optional in a real implementation. Tasks where each step's result determines the next step (genuine exploration, debugging) fit ReAct better, since the information needed to plan does not exist yet.<br><br><strong>How I'd choose:</strong> Plan-and-Execute for tasks with predictable structure — data pipelines, multi-step form filling, report generation — where you want cost control and human approval of the plan. ReAct for open-ended investigation. Many production systems use both: plan at the top level, ReAct within a step.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> a plan is a commitment, so a bad plan wastes the entire budget before failure surfaces. Validate the plan cheaply — schema-check step feasibility against available tools before executing any of it.` },

{ q: 'What is tool use (function calling), and how does it enable agents?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Tool use gives the model a set of function schemas — name, description, JSON-typed parameters — and lets it emit a structured call the runtime executes, returning the result into context. Models are explicitly fine-tuned for this, which is why native function calling is far more reliable than parsing tool calls out of free text.<br><br><strong>What it unlocks:</strong> current information (a model's weights are frozen; an API is not), exact computation (models are bad at arithmetic, good at writing calls to a calculator), private data behind access control, and <em>side effects</em> — the ability to change the world, which is what makes something an agent rather than a chatbot.<br><br><strong>The mechanic worth being precise about:</strong> the model does not execute anything. It emits a structured intent; your runtime decides whether to execute it. That gap is the entire security boundary — it is where you validate arguments, check authorization, and require human confirmation for consequential actions. Teams that auto-execute whatever the model emits have no boundary at all.<br><br><strong>Practical failure modes:</strong> hallucinated tools or parameters (mitigate with strict schema validation and clear errors fed back), wrong tool selection when many tools overlap, and unbounded outputs blowing the context window.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> tool results are untrusted input. A tool that fetches a web page or reads an email is an indirect prompt-injection vector — the returned content enters the same context as your instructions.` },

{ q: 'How do you design and define tools for an agent?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Design tools for the model's decision-making, not as a thin wrapper over your existing API. The two have different requirements.<br><br><strong>Fewer, higher-level tools beat many granular ones.</strong> Selection accuracy degrades with tool count and with overlap between descriptions — if two tools could plausibly serve a request, the model will sometimes pick wrong. Prefer one <code>search_orders(customer, date_range, status)</code> over five near-identical variants. Above roughly 10–20 tools, introduce a retrieval step that selects a relevant subset per query rather than listing them all.<br><br><strong>The description is the prompt.</strong> State what the tool does, <em>when to use it</em>, when <em>not</em> to, and what it returns. Ambiguity here is the single largest cause of wrong tool selection — more than model capability.<br><br><strong>Constrain parameters in the schema:</strong> enums over free strings, explicit formats for dates, required versus optional marked correctly, and sensible defaults. Every constraint you encode is a class of error the model cannot make.<br><br><strong>Design the return value for a reader with a token budget:</strong> structured, truncated, with the most decision-relevant fields first. Dumping a raw 50k-token API response destroys the context the reasoning depends on.<br><br><strong>Make errors instructive:</strong> "date must be YYYY-MM-DD, got '3rd March'" lets the model self-correct; a bare 400 does not.<br><br><strong>And make them safe by construction:</strong> scoped credentials, read-only where possible, idempotency keys on writes, and human confirmation for irreversible actions — enforced in the tool layer, not requested in the prompt.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between single-agent and multi-agent systems?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `A single agent has one model, one context, and one tool set. A multi-agent system splits work across specialized agents — typically an orchestrator delegating to sub-agents with distinct roles, tools, and their own contexts.<br><br><strong>The genuine motivations:</strong> <em>context isolation</em> is the strongest — a sub-agent can burn 100k tokens exploring and return a 500-token summary, keeping the orchestrator's context clean. <em>Parallelism</em> across independent subtasks cuts wall-clock time. <em>Specialization</em> lets each agent have a focused tool set and prompt, which improves tool-selection accuracy. And <em>separation of privilege</em> — a sub-agent that reads untrusted content can be given no write tools at all.<br><br><strong>The costs are systematically underestimated:</strong> coordination overhead often exceeds the benefit; information is lost at every hand-off because agents communicate through a lossy text interface; failures compound across agents and are much harder to debug, since there is no single trajectory to read; and token spend multiplies.<br><br><strong>My default:</strong> start single-agent. Most tasks that "obviously need a team of agents" are better served by one agent with good tools. Escalate to multi-agent when you have a concrete reason — a specific context-window problem, a measurable parallelism win, or a security boundary that requires separated privilege.<br><br><strong>The pattern that works best in practice</strong> is orchestrator-worker with a strict contract: workers get a narrow task and return structured results, and the orchestrator holds the plan. Free-form agent-to-agent conversation is where multi-agent systems go to die.` },

{ q: 'What is Model Context Protocol (MCP)?', tags: [], a: `MCP is an open protocol standardizing how LLM applications connect to external tools, data, and prompts. It defines a client-server model over JSON-RPC where servers expose <strong>tools</strong> (callable functions), <strong>resources</strong> (readable data), and <strong>prompts</strong> (reusable templates), and any MCP-compatible client can consume them.<br><br><strong>The problem it solves is combinatorial.</strong> Before it, every application built bespoke integrations for every data source — M applications × N sources = M×N integrations. MCP makes it M+N: write one server per source, and every client gets it. That is the whole value proposition, and it is a genuine one.<br><br><strong>Practically it means</strong> tool integrations become portable across agent frameworks and hosts, discovery is dynamic (a client can enumerate what a server offers at runtime rather than hardcoding schemas), and there is a shared vocabulary for capabilities beyond function calling.<br><br><strong>The engineering caveats to raise:</strong> MCP standardizes <em>transport and schema</em>, not tool quality — a badly described tool is just as badly described over MCP. Connecting many servers reintroduces the tool-count problem, since selection accuracy degrades as the combined tool list grows. And each server is a trust boundary: a third-party MCP server can return content that acts as an indirect prompt injection, and tool descriptions themselves are attacker-controllable text entering your context. Vet servers, scope credentials per server, and treat their output as untrusted.` },

{ q: 'What are AI sub-agents?', tags: [], a: `A sub-agent is an agent invoked by another agent as a tool: it receives a scoped task, runs its own loop with its own context and tool set, and returns a result to the caller.<br><br><strong>The dominant reason to use them is context management.</strong> A sub-agent can consume 100k tokens searching a codebase and return a 1k-token answer — the orchestrator never sees the intermediate noise. That keeps the main context clean and focused, which directly improves the orchestrator's reasoning, since attention degrades over long, cluttered contexts.<br><br><strong>The second reason is privilege separation.</strong> A sub-agent that processes untrusted content — a fetched web page, an uploaded document, an incoming email — can be given <em>no</em> write tools and no credentials, returning only structured extracted data. Injected instructions in that content then reach a context that cannot act on them. This is one of the few structurally sound defenses against indirect prompt injection.<br><br><strong>Third is parallelism</strong> across independent subtasks.<br><br><strong>The costs:</strong> each sub-agent is a full agent loop, so cost and latency multiply; the hand-off interface is lossy — the caller sees only what the sub-agent chose to report, so a sub-agent that misunderstands its task returns a confident wrong summary with no trace; and debugging spans multiple trajectories.<br><br><strong>Design rule:</strong> give sub-agents narrow, well-specified tasks with structured return schemas. Vague delegation ("figure out the auth system") produces vague results you cannot verify.` },

{ q: 'What are the types of agent memory?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Short-term / working memory:</strong> the active context window — recent steps, tool outputs, current plan. Fast, complete, and bounded; everything eventually falls out of it.<br><br><strong>Long-term memory:</strong> external storage retrieved on demand — a vector index for semantic recall, a key-value store for facts, a database for structured records. Unbounded but requires the agent to retrieve the right thing at the right time, which is itself a retrieval problem.<br><br><strong>Episodic memory:</strong> records of specific past events — what the agent did, what happened, what the user corrected. This is what enables "last time you tried X and it failed."<br><br><strong>Semantic memory:</strong> distilled general facts extracted from episodes — user preferences, domain knowledge, learned constraints.<br><br><strong>Procedural memory:</strong> learned how-to knowledge — in practice, accumulated instructions or few-shot examples of successful trajectories.<br><br><strong>The taxonomy is borrowed from cognitive science and can be over-applied.</strong> What matters in production is simpler: what is in context now, what is retrievable, and what is <em>always</em> injected regardless of relevance. That third category — durable facts and constraints — is the one teams omit, and it is why agents forget the user's plan tier at step 40.<br><br><strong>The hard part is not storage, it is writing.</strong> Deciding what is worth remembering, when to update a fact versus append a new one, and how to resolve contradictions between remembered and current information. Most memory systems fail on write policy, not read.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle agent failures and implement error recovery?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Classify failures, because they need different responses and treating them uniformly produces both wasted retries and unrecovered errors.<br><br><strong>Transient</strong> (timeout, rate limit, 503): retry with exponential backoff and jitter, in the tool layer, invisibly to the model. Do not spend model tokens on infrastructure flakiness.<br><br><strong>Malformed input</strong> (bad parameters, schema violation): return a <em>specific, instructive</em> error to the model — "date must be YYYY-MM-DD, got '3rd March'" — and let it self-correct. This works well and is the main reason error message design matters.<br><br><strong>Semantic failure</strong> (valid call, wrong result — searched the wrong index, retrieved nothing useful): the model must notice and replan, which requires the observation to actually convey that the result was empty or off-target rather than silently returning nothing.<br><br><strong>Terminal</strong> (permission denied, resource does not exist, budget exhausted): stop. Retrying is pure waste, and the agent must have a defined path to escalate or report failure honestly rather than fabricating success.<br><br><strong>The structural safeguards:</strong> hard step and token budgets, per-step timeouts, a cap on retries per tool per task, and checkpointing so a long trajectory can resume rather than restart.<br><br><strong>The failure mode that hurts most in production:</strong> silent success — the agent reports completion having accomplished nothing, because nothing verified the outcome. The fix is verification tools: after a write, read it back; after a claimed fix, run the test. An agent that cannot check its own work will confidently tell you it finished.` },

{ q: 'What is an agent loop, and how does it decide when to stop?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `The loop is: build context → model produces either a tool call or a final answer → if a tool call, execute it and append the observation → repeat. The model, not your code, decides each iteration's action.<br><br><strong>Termination has three sources.</strong> <em>Model-decided</em>: it emits a final answer instead of a tool call — the intended path, and unreliable on its own. <em>Condition-based</em>: your code checks a goal predicate (the test passes, the record exists, the schema validates) — far more trustworthy because it is verified rather than asserted. <em>Budget-based</em>: hard caps on steps, tokens, wall-clock time, or spend — the safety net that must always exist.<br><br><strong>Why the model's own stopping judgment is weak:</strong> nothing in the trajectory tells it "you are repeating yourself" unless you make that visible, and it has no reliable sense of whether it has actually succeeded. Agents both stop too early (declaring success without verifying) and never stop (retrying the same failing call).<br><br><strong>Loop detection specifically:</strong> hash recent (tool, arguments) pairs and detect repetition, then either inject that observation into context ("you have called this 3 times with the same arguments") or break. Injecting it works surprisingly well — the model usually changes strategy once it can see the repetition.<br><br><strong>The design rule:</strong> never ship an agent whose only stopping condition is the model's judgment. Budgets are not a fallback for unusual cases; they are the thing standing between a bad trajectory and an unbounded bill.` },

{ q: 'What is context engineering?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Context engineering is the discipline of deciding what information occupies the model's finite context at each step — what to include, in what form, in what order, and what to leave out. It generalizes prompt engineering from "write good instructions" to "manage a scarce resource."<br><br><strong>Why it is the central skill for agents:</strong> context is finite, attention degrades over length (the lost-in-the-middle effect is real and measurable), every token costs money and latency, and irrelevant content actively harms reasoning rather than being neutral. An agent's failures are frequently context failures — the right information was available and was not in the window, or was there and was buried.<br><br><strong>The levers:</strong> <em>selection</em> (retrieve only what this step needs, rather than everything that might be relevant), <em>compression</em> (summarize old steps, truncate tool outputs, strip boilerplate), <em>ordering</em> (critical instructions last, strongest evidence at the edges), <em>structure</em> (delimiters and consistent formatting so the model can parse roles), and <em>isolation</em> (push exploration into a sub-agent whose noise never enters the main context).<br><br><strong>The counterintuitive point worth making:</strong> more context often means worse results. Teams reach for a bigger window when the fix is retrieving less and better. A 200k window is a budget, not a target.<br><br><strong>And it is measurable:</strong> log the assembled context per step, track token composition by category, and run needle-in-a-haystack tests at your real context lengths. Context problems are invisible until you look at what the model actually received.` },

{ q: 'How does context compaction work?', tags: [], a: `Compaction reduces the token footprint of an agent's history when it approaches the context limit, so the trajectory can continue without losing the thread.<br><br><strong>The common strategies:</strong> <em>summarize</em> older turns into a compact narrative while keeping recent steps verbatim; <em>truncate</em> tool outputs to their decision-relevant fields (a 50k-token API response usually contains 200 useful tokens); <em>drop</em> entries known to be irrelevant, such as failed attempts already superseded; and <em>externalize</em> — write detail to a file or store and keep only a reference, so the agent can re-read on demand.<br><br><strong>The structure that works:</strong> a running summary of what has been done and learned, plus the last N steps in full, plus a <strong>never-compacted section</strong> holding the original task, hard constraints, and durable facts. That last part is essential — the most common compaction bug is losing the original objective, after which the agent optimizes for the summary's paraphrase of the task rather than the task.<br><br><strong>The failure mode:</strong> recursive summarization compounds error. Each pass re-encodes a lossy encoding, so specifics degrade into vagueness and then into confident inaccuracy. Summarize from the original entries where possible rather than summarizing summaries, and keep extracted facts as verbatim quotes.<br><br><strong>Second-order:</strong> compaction is also a cache invalidation event — rewriting the prefix destroys prompt-cache hits, so a naive implementation that compacts every step is expensive. Compact at thresholds, not continuously.` },

{ q: 'What is loop engineering?', tags: [], a: `Loop engineering is the design of the agent's iteration structure — what happens each cycle, what is carried forward, what triggers termination, and how errors and repetition are handled. It is the control-flow half of harness engineering.<br><br><strong>The decisions that matter:</strong><br>• <em>What enters context each iteration</em> — full history, or a compacted view, or a curated working set. This is the largest driver of both cost and quality.<br>• <em>Termination conditions</em> — model-decided, condition-verified, and budget-enforced, layered rather than chosen.<br>• <em>Progress detection</em> — hashing recent (tool, args) pairs to catch repetition, and tracking whether state actually changed between steps. Without this, an agent can spend its entire budget going nowhere.<br>• <em>Error semantics</em> — which failures retry silently in the tool layer, which surface to the model for self-correction, which terminate.<br>• <em>Checkpointing</em> — persisting state so a long trajectory can resume rather than restart, which matters as soon as runs exceed a few minutes.<br><br><strong>The framing worth stating:</strong> the loop is where cost and safety are actually controlled. A model choosing badly for 5 steps is a bad answer; a model choosing badly for 500 steps is an incident. Budgets, timeouts, and progress checks are not defensive extras — they are the loop's core contract.<br><br><strong>And instrument it:</strong> log every step with tool, arguments, tokens, latency, and outcome. You cannot debug an agent by reading its final answer; you debug it by reading the trajectory.` },

{ q: 'What is graph engineering in agent systems?', tags: [], a: `Graph engineering models an agent system as an explicit graph — nodes are steps (an LLM call, a tool, a deterministic function), edges are transitions, and the transitions can be conditional on state. LangGraph is the common implementation, but the idea is framework-independent.<br><br><strong>What it buys over a free-form loop:</strong> the control flow becomes <em>inspectable and testable</em>. You can see every reachable path, unit-test individual nodes, enforce that certain edges require human approval, and reason about termination. State is explicit and typed rather than implicit in a growing message list.<br><br>It also enables cycles with structure — a node can route back for retry or refinement, but through a defined edge with a condition, rather than the model simply deciding to try again.<br><br><strong>The practical advantages:</strong> checkpointing at node boundaries (so long runs resume rather than restart), human-in-the-loop as a first-class interrupt at a specific node, parallel fan-out and join, and observability that maps cleanly onto the graph.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff:</strong> you trade autonomy for control. A graph constrains what the agent can do, which is exactly the point for production reliability and exactly the limitation when the task genuinely requires open-ended exploration.<br><br><strong>How I'd frame the choice:</strong> most production "agents" are better as graphs with a few model-decided branches than as a free-running ReAct loop. Put the model where judgment is genuinely needed and make everything else deterministic — that is where reliability comes from.` },

{ q: 'How do AI agents communicate?', tags: [], a: `Within a system, agents almost always communicate through <strong>natural language plus structured payloads</strong> — an orchestrator passes a task description and receives a result, ideally against a defined schema rather than free prose.<br><br><strong>The common topologies:</strong> <em>orchestrator-worker</em> (a supervisor delegates and aggregates — by far the most reliable in production); <em>sequential pipeline</em> (each agent's output feeds the next); <em>blackboard</em> (agents read and write a shared state store rather than messaging each other directly, which decouples them and gives a single inspectable state); and <em>free-form conversation</em> (agents talk to each other in a group chat).<br><br><strong>The engineering reality:</strong> free-form agent conversation is where multi-agent systems fail. It produces unbounded token spend, agents agreeing with each other into confident nonsense, no clear termination, and trajectories nobody can debug. Structured hand-offs with explicit schemas work; open dialogue mostly does not.<br><br><strong>The core problem is lossy hand-off.</strong> Everything an agent knows is compressed into a text message the next agent receives with no ability to ask clarifying questions of the original context. Information degrades at every boundary — which is the strongest argument for fewer agents and richer shared state.<br><br><strong>Emerging standards:</strong> MCP standardizes agent-to-tool connections; agent-to-agent protocols (A2A and similar) aim to standardize cross-system agent interaction. Useful for interoperability, but they do not solve the hand-off fidelity problem, which is a design issue rather than a transport one.` },

{ q: 'What are agent skills?', tags: [], a: `A skill is a packaged unit of capability an agent can load on demand — typically instructions plus optional scripts, resources, and tool definitions, describing how to perform a specific kind of task.<br><br><strong>The problem they solve is context economics.</strong> You cannot put every procedure the agent might need into the system prompt: it would exceed the window, and irrelevant instructions degrade performance on the current task. Skills invert this — the agent sees a short catalog of names and descriptions, and loads the full instructions only when a task matches. This is progressive disclosure applied to capability.<br><br><strong>Why this matters more than it sounds:</strong> it makes agent capability <em>composable and maintainable</em>. A procedure lives in one versioned file rather than being duplicated across prompts, it can be updated without touching the agent, and it can be shared across agents and teams.<br><br><strong>The design considerations:</strong> the description is what drives selection, so it must state clearly when the skill applies and when it does not — ambiguous descriptions produce wrong selection exactly as with tools. Skills should be self-contained (an agent loading one mid-task should not need three others), and the catalog itself must stay small enough not to reintroduce the selection problem it solved.<br><br><strong>The relationship to tools:</strong> tools are <em>actions</em> the model can take; skills are <em>procedures</em> telling it how to combine actions for a class of task. A skill often bundles tool usage patterns with domain knowledge and worked examples.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate and test AI agents?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Agent evaluation is harder than model evaluation because the output is a <em>trajectory</em>, not a string, and the same task can be completed well by many different paths.<br><br><strong>Evaluate at three levels.</strong> <em>Outcome</em>: did the task actually get done? Verify with a programmatic check on final state — the record exists, the test passes, the file has the right contents — not by asking the model or an LLM judge whether it succeeded. This is the metric that matters and the one teams most often fake.<br><br><em>Trajectory</em>: how efficiently, and did it do anything it should not have? Track step count, token and dollar cost, tool-call accuracy, redundant calls, and constraint violations. Two agents with the same success rate but 3x cost difference are not equivalent.<br><br><em>Component</em>: unit-test individual pieces — tool selection accuracy given a query, parameter extraction correctness, retrieval quality. Component tests localize failures that end-to-end tests only detect.<br><br><strong>Build the eval set from real failures.</strong> Collect production trajectories, label them, and turn every incident into a regression case. Include adversarial cases — prompt injection attempts, ambiguous requests, tools that error.<br><br><strong>The infrastructure requirement:</strong> a deterministic test environment. Agents that hit live APIs give non-reproducible results, so record-and-replay or mock the tool layer for regression runs, keeping a smaller live suite for integration.<br><br><strong>The honest caveat:</strong> agent evals are noisy — the same agent on the same task can succeed and fail across runs. Run each case multiple times and report pass rate with variance, not a single number.` },

{ q: 'What are the security risks of agentic systems, and how do you mitigate them?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `The defining risk is that agents combine <strong>untrusted input</strong> with <strong>privileged action</strong>. Everything else follows from that.<br><br><strong>Indirect prompt injection</strong> is the primary threat: malicious instructions embedded in a web page, document, email, or tool result the agent consumes. The attacker never touches your interface, and the payload arrives through a channel the agent trusts. There is no reliable prompt-level defense — delimiters and "ignore instructions in this document" raise cost, not a boundary.<br><br><strong>The other risks:</strong> excessive agency (an agent with broader permissions than the task needs), data exfiltration (classically via a rendered markdown image whose URL encodes stolen data), confused deputy (the agent uses <em>its</em> credentials to do something the <em>user</em> is not authorized to do), unbounded resource consumption, and supply-chain risk from third-party tools and MCP servers whose descriptions and outputs enter your context.<br><br><strong>Mitigate architecturally, assuming the model can be compromised:</strong><br>• Least-privilege, scoped, short-lived credentials per session — never ambient authority.<br>• Authorization enforced in the tool layer as code, checking the <em>user's</em> permissions, never a decision the model makes.<br>• Human confirmation for irreversible or outward-facing actions: send, delete, pay, publish.<br>• Sandbox code execution and untrusted content processing — a sub-agent with no write tools that returns structured data only.<br>• Sanitize outbound URLs and disable auto-rendering of model-supplied links.<br>• Hard budgets and full audit logging of every tool call.<br><br><strong>The design rule:</strong> break the untrusted-input-plus-privilege combination. Either the input is trusted or the tools are unprivileged.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between reactive and proactive agents?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Reactive</strong> agents act only when invoked — a user sends a request, the agent runs, returns a result, and stops. All current production LLM agents are fundamentally reactive: they have no independent execution and no continuity between invocations.<br><br><strong>Proactive</strong> agents initiate action based on triggers rather than requests — scheduled runs, event subscriptions, monitored thresholds, or their own assessment that something needs doing. "Watch this repository and open a PR when tests break" is proactive.<br><br><strong>In practice the distinction is architectural, not about the model.</strong> Proactivity comes from the harness: a scheduler, an event bus, a watcher process that wakes the agent. The model is identical; what changes is who decides when it runs.<br><br><strong>Why this matters more than it sounds:</strong> proactive agents remove the human from the initiation step, which removes a natural checkpoint. A reactive agent's damage is bounded by how often someone asks it to do something; a proactive one can act repeatedly on a wrong premise with nobody watching. That changes the safety requirements — spend caps, action allowlists, and mandatory human approval for consequential steps go from good practice to load-bearing.<br><br><strong>The practical failure mode:</strong> proactive agents that fire on noisy triggers generate alert fatigue, and their output stops being read — at which point their errors accumulate unnoticed. Trigger precision matters as much as agent quality, and it is worth measuring what fraction of proactive runs produced something a human acted on.` },

{ q: 'How do you manage token consumption and cost in long-running agent workflows?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Measure the composition first: log tokens per step split by category — system prompt, tool definitions, history, tool outputs, and generation. Agent cost is almost always dominated by one or two of these, and teams optimize the wrong one without this breakdown.<br><br><strong>The usual culprit is history growth.</strong> Every step re-sends the entire trajectory, so cost grows quadratically with steps if nothing is compacted. Fix with compaction — summarize old steps, keep recent ones verbatim, and never compact the task statement and hard constraints.<br><br><strong>The second is tool output.</strong> Truncate aggressively at the tool layer, returning decision-relevant fields rather than raw payloads. A 50k-token API response usually contains a few hundred useful tokens, and the rest actively degrades reasoning.<br><br><strong>Prompt caching is the highest-leverage single change:</strong> keep the system prompt and tool definitions byte-identical and at the front so the prefix is cached across steps. This alone can cut agent cost dramatically, and it is why compaction should happen at thresholds rather than every step (each rewrite invalidates the cache).<br><br><strong>Structural moves:</strong> route simple steps to a smaller model; push exploration into sub-agents that return summaries; replace model-decided steps with deterministic code wherever the logic is fixed.<br><br><strong>Enforce, do not just optimize:</strong> hard token and dollar budgets per task, checked before each step, with graceful degradation — return partial results rather than dying. Track cost per completed task, not cost per call; an agent that is cheap per step but never finishes is the expensive one.` },

{ q: 'What is the human-in-the-loop pattern, and when is it needed?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Human-in-the-loop inserts a person at a decision point — approving a plan, confirming an action, resolving ambiguity, or reviewing output before it takes effect.<br><br><strong>When it is required rather than optional:</strong> irreversible actions (delete, send, publish, pay, deploy), actions crossing a trust boundary to third parties, high-cost operations, decisions with legal or regulatory weight, and any point where the agent's confidence is low or its evidence is contradictory. The test I'd apply: if this action were wrong, could we undo it cheaply? If not, a human confirms.<br><br><strong>The implementation patterns:</strong> <em>approve-before-execute</em> (agent proposes, human confirms — the standard for consequential tool calls), <em>plan approval</em> (review the whole plan up front, which is cheaper than per-step confirmation for long tasks), <em>escalation</em> (agent hands off when it detects it is out of scope or stuck), and <em>post-hoc review</em> with the ability to roll back, appropriate for lower-stakes actions.<br><br><strong>Design it so the human can actually decide.</strong> A confirmation prompt showing a raw JSON tool call is not a control — it is a rubber stamp. Show what will change, in domain terms, with the reasoning and the affected scope. And make rejection informative: capture <em>why</em> so it becomes training or eval data.<br><br><strong>The failure mode to design against:</strong> approval fatigue. Confirm everything and humans click through blindly, which is worse than no gate because it manufactures false assurance. Reserve interrupts for genuinely consequential decisions and let the rest run.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement guardrails to prevent harmful agent actions?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Guardrails must be <strong>outside</strong> the model. Anything expressed as an instruction is a preference the model may or may not honor, and adversarial input can override it. The rule: if it matters, it is enforced in code.<br><br><strong>Layer them:</strong><br>• <em>Input</em> — classify and reject out-of-scope or malicious requests before the loop starts; treat all retrieved and tool-returned content as untrusted data.<br>• <em>Action</em> — the primary layer. Allowlist tools per agent role; validate arguments against strict schemas; enforce authorization in the tool implementation against the <em>user's</em> permissions; require confirmation for irreversible operations; apply rate and spend limits.<br>• <em>Environment</em> — sandbox code execution with no network and an ephemeral filesystem; use read-only credentials by default; scope database access to specific tables and row-level policies rather than granting broad access.<br>• <em>Output</em> — scan for PII and secrets before returning; sanitize URLs and disable auto-rendering of model-supplied links to block exfiltration.<br>• <em>Runtime</em> — step, token, and dollar budgets; anomaly detection on unusual tool-call patterns; full audit logging.<br><br><strong>Design for blast radius, not prevention.</strong> Assume some action will be wrong and make wrong actions cheap: soft deletes, staged writes with review, idempotency keys, and a rollback path. That is more robust than trying to enumerate every harmful action in advance.<br><br><strong>And test the guardrails themselves</strong> — red-team with injection attempts and adversarial tasks as part of the eval suite. A guardrail nobody has tried to break is an assumption.` },

{ q: 'What is agent reflection, and how does it improve performance?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Reflection has the agent critique its own output and revise — generate, evaluate against criteria, then improve based on that critique. Reflexion adds persistence: the self-critique is stored in memory so later attempts avoid repeating the same mistake.<br><br><strong>Why it works when it works:</strong> generating and evaluating are different tasks, and models are often better at spotting a flaw in existing text than at avoiding it during generation. Separating the two gives the model a second pass with the output in context, where errors are easier to see than to prevent.<br><br><strong>The critical dependency is the feedback signal.</strong> Reflection is genuinely effective with <em>external, verifiable</em> feedback — test results, compiler errors, schema validation, execution output. There the critique is grounded in fact and improvement is reliable.<br><br><strong>Pure self-critique is much weaker,</strong> and this is the point that separates a strong answer. Research shows models often cannot detect their own reasoning errors — if the model had the knowledge to identify the mistake, it frequently would not have made it. Worse, self-critique can degrade correct answers: the model, asked to find problems, invents them and "fixes" something that was right. Studies on self-correction without external feedback find neutral-to-negative effects on reasoning tasks.<br><br><strong>How I'd use it:</strong> reflect against real signals — run the tests, validate the schema, execute the code. Cap iterations (returns flatten fast, usually by 2–3). And measure whether revision actually improves your eval scores, because on many tasks it will not, and you will have paid double for it.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between code-generating and tool-calling agents?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Tool-calling agents</strong> select from a fixed set of predefined functions, emitting structured arguments. <strong>Code-generating agents</strong> (CodeAct-style) write code that is executed in a sandbox, calling libraries and APIs directly.<br><br><strong>Why code generation is more expressive:</strong> a single code block can express loops, conditionals, composition, and data transformation that would take many sequential tool calls — and each tool call is a full model round-trip. Filtering 10,000 records and aggregating takes one code block or hundreds of tool calls. Code also composes naturally: the output of one call feeds the next without passing through the model's context, which saves enormous token spend.<br><br><strong>Why tool calling is safer and more predictable:</strong> the action space is enumerable, so you can audit, allowlist, validate arguments, and enforce authorization per tool. Failures are localized and legible. Code execution has an unbounded action space by definition, which is precisely what makes sandboxing mandatory rather than optional.<br><br><strong>How I'd decide:</strong> tool calling for actions with real-world side effects, where the security boundary and auditability matter — payments, writes to production systems, sending communications. Code generation for data manipulation, analysis, and computation, where expressiveness pays and the sandbox contains the risk.<br><br><strong>The hybrid is common and sensible:</strong> expose tools for privileged side effects and a sandboxed code interpreter for computation. The sandbox needs real isolation — no network, ephemeral filesystem, resource limits, and no credentials — because generated code is untrusted code.` },

{ q: 'How do you handle multi-modal inputs and outputs in agentic systems?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Inputs:</strong> route by modality at the boundary. Images go to a vision-capable model directly, or through a VLM that produces a structured text description the rest of the pipeline consumes. Audio goes through transcription. Documents go through a layout-aware parser or a VLM page-transcription pass. The design question is whether to keep the raw modality in context (expensive, higher fidelity) or convert to text early (cheaper, composable with all your existing text tooling).<br><br><strong>My default is convert early</strong> for anything that will be retrieved, searched, or compared — text is the universal interchange format, and it keeps hybrid search, chunking, and evaluation working unchanged. Keep the raw asset referenced so the agent can look at it directly when detail matters.<br><br><strong>Outputs:</strong> generation of non-text modalities is a tool call — image generation, TTS, chart rendering. The agent decides what to produce and the tool produces it, which keeps the action auditable.<br><br><strong>The practical issues:</strong> images consume large token counts (a high-res image can cost thousands), so resolution and tiling policy is a real cost lever; multi-modal context blows up quickly in long trajectories, so old images should be replaced with their text descriptions during compaction; and error handling differs — a failed OCR returns plausible garbage rather than an error, so validation matters more.<br><br><strong>Security note:</strong> images are an injection vector — text embedded in an image can carry instructions that OCR or a VLM faithfully transfers into context. Treat transcribed content as untrusted, exactly like a fetched web page.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement state management in complex agent workflows?', tags: [], a: `Make state <strong>explicit and external</strong> rather than implicit in a growing message list. The message history is a log, not a state store — deriving current state by re-reading the whole conversation is fragile, expensive, and breaks under compaction.<br><br><strong>The structure that works:</strong> a typed state object holding the task, constraints, accumulated results, current plan, and step count — separate from the message history. The model reads a rendered view of it; your code owns and mutates it. LangGraph formalizes exactly this, but the pattern matters more than the framework.<br><br><strong>Persist and checkpoint</strong> at step boundaries. Long-running agents fail — timeouts, rate limits, crashes — and restarting a 40-step trajectory from zero is both expensive and often non-idempotent (it re-sends the email it already sent). Checkpointing enables resume, and it enables human-in-the-loop as an interrupt: pause, wait for approval, continue from saved state.<br><br><strong>Handle idempotency deliberately:</strong> every side-effecting tool should take an idempotency key derived from the step, so retries and resumes do not duplicate actions. This is the difference between a resumable agent and one that double-charges a customer.<br><br><strong>For multi-agent systems,</strong> prefer a shared state store (blackboard) over message passing — it gives one inspectable source of truth rather than state scattered across conversation histories, and it avoids the lossy hand-off problem.<br><br><strong>And version the state schema.</strong> In-flight trajectories outlive deploys, so a schema change must not break agents mid-run.` },

{ q: 'How do you build a customer support agent with escalation logic?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Architecture:</strong> retrieval over your knowledge base and past tickets, tools for account lookup and order status, a conversation memory holding customer context, and an escalation path to a human queue with full transcript hand-off.<br><br><strong>Escalation should be triggered by rules, not left to the model's judgment alone.</strong> Deterministic triggers: explicit customer request for a human, detected frustration or repeated failed attempts (two unsuccessful resolution turns is a reasonable threshold), account or transaction value above a limit, legal/compliance keywords (chargeback, lawsuit, data deletion), refunds or account changes above a policy threshold, and low retrieval confidence — if the reranker score is below threshold, the agent has no grounded answer and should not improvise one.<br><br><strong>Model-side triggers</strong> supplement these: an explicit <code>escalate_to_human(reason)</code> tool the model can call, with the reason logged. Make escalation an easy, first-class action rather than a failure — agents that treat handing off as losing will hallucinate instead.<br><br><strong>Hand off well:</strong> pass the full transcript, the customer's account context, what the agent already tried, and the trigger reason. An escalation that makes the customer repeat themselves is worse than no agent.<br><br><strong>The guardrails:</strong> answer only from retrieved policy with citations; never let the model invent policy, commitments, or compensation amounts; require confirmation for any account-modifying action; and enforce authorization on account lookups so the agent cannot be talked into reading another customer's data.<br><br><strong>Measure:</strong> containment rate, escalation precision, CSAT split by resolved-vs-escalated, and — most importantly — the rate of confidently wrong answers, which is the failure that destroys trust.` },

{ q: 'What is agent orchestration, and how do you implement it?', tags: [], a: `Orchestration is the coordination layer deciding which agent or step runs when, what context it receives, how results combine, and how failures are handled. It is the difference between a collection of agents and a system.<br><br><strong>The common patterns:</strong> <em>router</em> (classify the request and dispatch to the right specialist — cheapest and often sufficient), <em>orchestrator-worker</em> (a supervisor decomposes, delegates to sub-agents, and aggregates — the most reliable multi-agent pattern in production), <em>sequential pipeline</em> (fixed stages, each output feeding the next), and <em>graph</em> (explicit nodes and conditional edges, which is orchestration made inspectable).<br><br><strong>Implementation essentials:</strong> a typed shared state rather than state scattered across message histories; structured contracts between steps so hand-offs are validatable; checkpointing at boundaries for resume and human approval; parallel fan-out with a join for independent subtasks; and budgets enforced at the orchestrator level, since per-agent budgets do not bound the total.<br><br><strong>The judgment call:</strong> put the model where judgment is genuinely required and make everything else deterministic code. Most production systems benefit from an orchestration layer that is a plain state machine with a few model-decided branches — not an LLM deciding every transition. Model-driven orchestration is more flexible and much harder to test, debug, and cost-bound.<br><br><strong>Observability is non-negotiable:</strong> trace the full execution across agents with a shared correlation id. Without distributed tracing you cannot answer "why did this task fail" in a multi-agent system, because there is no single trajectory to read.` },

{ q: 'How do you build a code execution agent safely?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Treat generated code as untrusted code, because that is exactly what it is. The model can be induced to write anything, so the sandbox is the only real control.<br><br><strong>Isolation, strongest first:</strong> a microVM (Firecracker, gVisor) or a per-execution container with a fresh filesystem. Do not rely on language-level sandboxing — Python's <code>exec</code> with restricted builtins is escapable and has been broken repeatedly. Process isolation is not isolation.<br><br><strong>The controls that matter:</strong><br>• <em>No network by default</em> — this blocks exfiltration and remote payload fetching in one step. Allowlist specific hosts if genuinely required.<br>• <em>No credentials in the environment</em> — no cloud metadata endpoint access, no mounted secrets, no ambient cloud identity. Metadata-service access from a sandbox is a classic privilege-escalation path.<br>• <em>Resource limits</em> — CPU, memory, disk, process count, and a hard wall-clock timeout, enforced by the runtime rather than by the code.<br>• <em>Ephemeral filesystem</em> — destroyed after each execution, with explicit read-only mounts for input data.<br>• <em>Non-root user</em>, dropped capabilities, and seccomp restrictions on syscalls.<br><br><strong>Data flow:</strong> pass inputs in explicitly and capture outputs (stdout, files, return values) rather than giving the sandbox access to your systems. If it needs data, mount a copy.<br><br><strong>Operationally:</strong> log every execution with the code and result for audit, rate-limit executions per session, and cap total compute per task. And return execution errors to the model as instructive observations — that feedback loop is what makes code agents effective, since the failing test or traceback is exactly the grounded signal reflection needs.` },

{ q: 'Your agent is stuck in an infinite loop. How do you detect and break it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Detect</strong> by hashing recent (tool name, normalized arguments) pairs and looking for repetition — the same call three times, or a short cycle of alternating calls, means no progress. Complement this with state-based detection: if nothing observable changed after N steps (no new information, no state mutation), the agent is spinning regardless of whether the calls look identical.<br><br><strong>Break it in escalating order:</strong><br>1. <em>Inject the observation</em> — tell the model explicitly: "You have called <code>search</code> with these arguments 3 times and received the same result. Try a different approach or report that you cannot complete this." This works surprisingly often, because the model genuinely cannot see the repetition otherwise; nothing in its context flags it.<br>2. <em>Force a strategy change</em> — remove the repeated tool from the available set for the next step.<br>3. <em>Hard stop</em> at the step or token budget, returning partial results rather than dying silently.<br><br><strong>The root causes worth fixing rather than patching:</strong> a tool returning an unhelpful or empty result the model does not recognize as terminal ("no results found" needs to be explicit, not an empty list); a goal the model cannot verify it has achieved, so it keeps trying; and missing information the agent has no path to obtain, where the correct behavior is to escalate rather than retry.<br><br><strong>The non-negotiable:</strong> every agent ships with hard step, token, wall-clock, and spend budgets. Loop detection is an optimization; budgets are what stand between a bad trajectory and an unbounded bill.` },

{ q: 'Your agent gets conflicting answers from different tools. How does it reconcile them?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Do not leave arbitration to the model's unassisted judgment — it will pick one confidently and give no indication that a conflict existed. That is the failure mode to design against.<br><br><strong>Establish source authority explicitly.</strong> Rank your tools: a transactional database is authoritative over a cached search index, which is authoritative over a wiki, which is authoritative over a web search. Encode this in the tool descriptions and in the reconciliation logic, so "the order database says shipped, the tracking API says pending" resolves by rule rather than by vibes.<br><br><strong>Distinguish the conflict types,</strong> because most are not real disagreements: <em>staleness</em> (one source is simply older — attach and compare timestamps), <em>scope</em> (both correct for different entities, regions, or time windows — usually a query construction bug), and <em>genuine contradiction</em> between equally authoritative sources.<br><br><strong>For genuine conflicts:</strong> the agent should surface the disagreement with both values and their sources rather than silently choosing, and escalate when the decision matters. In a support or financial context, presenting "system A says X, system B says Y" is the correct output — inventing a resolution is not.<br><br><strong>Detect it programmatically</strong> rather than hoping the model notices: when two tools return values for the same field, compare them in code and inject an explicit conflict notice into context.<br><br><strong>The durable fix is upstream.</strong> Persistent tool disagreement is a data-consistency problem in your systems, and every detected conflict should be logged as a data-quality signal — the agent is surfacing a real defect, not just having a bad day.` },

{ q: 'Your agent burns too many tokens per task. How do you reduce consumption?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Profile before optimizing: log per-step tokens split by system prompt, tool definitions, history, tool outputs, and generation. Agent spend is nearly always dominated by one or two categories, and the intuitive guess is usually wrong.<br><br><strong>History re-sending is the most common cause.</strong> Every step re-sends the full trajectory, so cost grows quadratically in step count. Fix with compaction: summarize old steps, keep recent ones verbatim, and never compact the task statement or hard constraints.<br><br><strong>Tool output is the second.</strong> Truncate at the tool layer to decision-relevant fields — a raw 50k-token API response contains a few hundred useful tokens, and the rest both costs money and degrades reasoning through context dilution. This is usually the single biggest win.<br><br><strong>Prompt caching is the highest-leverage change overall:</strong> keep the system prompt and tool definitions byte-identical and at the front of the prompt so the prefix is cached across every step. Large cost reduction for near-zero effort — and a reason to compact at thresholds rather than continuously, since each rewrite invalidates the cache.<br><br><strong>Structural reductions:</strong> replace model-decided steps with deterministic code wherever the logic is actually fixed; route simple steps to a smaller model; push exploration into sub-agents that return summaries instead of full trajectories; and reduce tool count, since tool definitions are re-sent every step.<br><br><strong>The metric to optimize:</strong> cost per <em>completed</em> task, not cost per call. An agent that is cheap per step but frequently fails and retries is the expensive one, and per-call optimization can make it worse.` },

{ q: 'Your agent keeps exceeding its budget per task. How do you enforce limits?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Enforce in the harness, checked before each step — not as an instruction to the model, which cannot count its own spend and has no incentive to stop.<br><br><strong>Track multiple budgets simultaneously,</strong> because they bind differently: cumulative tokens, dollar cost (weighted by model, since a step on a frontier model costs 20x one on a small model), step count, wall-clock time, and per-tool call counts (an agent making 400 search calls is pathological regardless of token count).<br><br><strong>Degrade gracefully rather than dying.</strong> At a soft threshold (say 70%), inject a notice into context: "you have used 70% of your budget; prioritize completing the task and return what you have." Models respond to this reasonably well. At the hard limit, terminate and return partial results with an explicit incomplete status — never a fabricated success.<br><br><strong>Then fix why it is exceeding.</strong> Budget overruns are usually a symptom: loops with no progress detection, tool outputs that are not truncated, retrieval returning nothing so the agent keeps trying, or a task genuinely beyond the agent's capability that should be escalated rather than retried. Read the trajectories of over-budget runs — the pattern is normally obvious and structural.<br><br><strong>Set budgets from data:</strong> measure the token distribution of <em>successful</em> completions and set the cap above the 95th percentile. A budget below the natural cost of success just produces expensive failures.<br><br><strong>And enforce at the orchestrator level</strong> in multi-agent systems — per-agent budgets do not bound the total, and a supervisor spawning sub-agents can multiply spend without any single component exceeding its own limit.` },

{ q: 'Your agent hallucinates tool capabilities and passes wrong inputs. How do you fix it?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Validate strictly and return instructive errors</strong> — this is the mechanism that actually works. Enforce the JSON schema at the tool boundary; on violation, return a message naming the specific problem and the correct format ("<code>date</code> must be YYYY-MM-DD, received '3rd March'; valid values for <code>status</code> are: open, closed, pending"). Models self-correct reliably from specific errors and not at all from a bare 400.<br><br><strong>Prevent structurally where you can:</strong> use constrained decoding or the provider's strict function-calling mode so arguments are guaranteed schema-valid at generation time. Use enums instead of free strings, explicit formats, and required/optional marked correctly. Every constraint encoded in the schema is a class of error the model cannot make.<br><br><strong>Fix the descriptions, since hallucinated capability is usually a description failure.</strong> If the model invents a <code>filter_by_region</code> parameter, it is because the tool's description implies filtering is possible. State precisely what the tool does, what it does <em>not</em> do, and what it returns. Include a worked example call in the description — demonstrations outperform prose.<br><br><strong>Reduce ambiguity across tools:</strong> if two tools have overlapping purposes, the model will conflate their parameters. Consolidate or sharpen the boundary between them.<br><br><strong>Instrument it:</strong> log every schema-validation failure with the attempted arguments. That log is the highest-signal dataset you have for improving tool definitions — a recurring wrong parameter almost always points at a specific ambiguous phrase in the description, and fixing the phrase fixes the class.` },

{ q: 'Your agent deleted a production database. How do you prevent irreversible actions?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `The premise is the lesson: <strong>the agent should never have held that capability.</strong> No amount of prompting prevents this — the fix is that the credential could not perform the action.<br><br><strong>Least privilege, enforced by the credential:</strong> read-only database users by default; write access scoped to specific tables with row-level policies; no DDL permissions ever; separate credentials per agent role, short-lived and scoped to the session. If the agent physically cannot issue a <code>DROP</code>, no prompt can make it happen.<br><br><strong>Classify actions and gate the destructive ones:</strong> maintain an explicit list of irreversible operations — delete, drop, truncate, send, publish, pay, deploy — that require human confirmation showing exactly what will change, in domain terms, before execution. Everything else runs freely.<br><br><strong>Design for reversibility so mistakes are cheap:</strong> soft deletes with a retention window instead of hard deletes; staged writes to a review area; transactions with explicit commit; idempotency keys so retries do not duplicate; and tested backups with a known restore time.<br><br><strong>Environment separation:</strong> agents get non-production by default, and production access requires explicit, audited elevation. A large fraction of these incidents are an agent pointed at the wrong environment because the connection string was in scope.<br><br><strong>And instrument:</strong> log every tool call with arguments and the resulting change, alert on unusual patterns (bulk deletes, sudden write volume), and rate-limit destructive operations so a runaway loop hits a wall before it finishes.<br><br><strong>The framing:</strong> assume the model will eventually attempt something wrong, and engineer so the blast radius is small — prevention by capability, not by instruction.` },

{ q: 'Your agent has many tools but keeps picking the wrong one. How do you improve selection?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Selection accuracy degrades with tool count and, more sharply, with <em>overlap</em> between tool purposes. Diagnose which you have by logging wrong selections and checking whether the chosen and correct tools have similar descriptions — they usually do.<br><br><strong>Fix the descriptions first, since this is the largest cause.</strong> Each description should state what the tool does, <em>when to use it</em>, <em>when not to</em>, and what it returns — with an explicit contrast where confusion exists ("use <code>search_orders</code> for historical orders; use <code>get_order_status</code> only when you already have an order ID"). Ambiguity here outweighs model capability.<br><br><strong>Consolidate.</strong> Five overlapping search tools should usually be one tool with parameters. Fewer, higher-level tools beat many granular ones because each additional tool adds a chance of confusion and re-sends its definition every step.<br><br><strong>Above roughly 10–20 tools, add retrieval:</strong> embed the tool descriptions, retrieve the top-k relevant to the current query, and present only those. This scales to hundreds of tools and is the standard fix — it also cuts token cost, since definitions are no longer all re-sent per step.<br><br><strong>Other levers:</strong> group tools by namespace and route to a category first, then a tool within it; add few-shot examples of correct selection for the historically confused pairs; and use a stronger model for the selection step specifically, since it is disproportionately sensitive to capability.<br><br><strong>Measure it as a unit test:</strong> a labeled set of (query → correct tool) pairs, run on every change. Tool selection is testable in isolation, and treating it as such makes this tractable rather than anecdotal.` },

{ q: 'Your agent takes too long to complete a task. How do you speed it up?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `Profile the breakdown: model inference time per step, tool latency, and step count. Agent latency is dominated by <em>number of sequential steps</em> in most systems — each step is a full round-trip — so reducing steps beats optimizing any single one.<br><br><strong>Reduce steps:</strong> give the model higher-level tools so one call does what previously took five (a <code>search_and_summarize</code> rather than search, then fetch, then read); replace model-decided steps with deterministic code where the logic is actually fixed; and improve tool descriptions so it stops wasting steps on wrong choices and retries.<br><br><strong>Parallelize:</strong> execute independent tool calls concurrently rather than serially — most frameworks support parallel tool calls and most implementations do not use them. Fan out sub-agents for independent subtasks.<br><br><strong>Cut per-step latency:</strong> smaller/faster models for simple steps (classification, extraction, routing) with escalation only for hard reasoning; prompt caching to slash time-to-first-token on the repeated prefix; shorter contexts, since prefill scales with prompt length; and streaming so the user sees progress even when total time is unchanged.<br><br><strong>Fix tool latency:</strong> often the real culprit — add caching, set aggressive timeouts, and run slow tools in the background where the agent can continue.<br><br><strong>The UX lever that is usually the biggest win:</strong> stream intermediate progress. A 30-second task that shows what it is doing feels dramatically better than a 20-second silent one, and it also lets users cancel early when the agent is off track.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff:</strong> nearly all of these trade quality for speed. Measure task success rate alongside latency, or you will ship a fast agent that fails more.` },

{ q: 'Your LLM selects the right tool but extracts wrong parameters. How do you fix it?', tags: [], a: `Parameter extraction fails for different reasons than tool selection, so diagnose specifically — log the user input, the extracted arguments, and the correct arguments to see the pattern.<br><br><strong>Constrain the schema harder.</strong> Most extraction errors are format or value errors that a tighter schema prevents: enums instead of free strings, explicit date formats with a description, numeric ranges, required versus optional marked correctly, and sensible defaults so the model is not inventing values for fields it should omit. Use strict function-calling or constrained decoding so arguments are schema-valid at generation time rather than validated after.<br><br><strong>Improve parameter descriptions</strong> — each field needs its own description, not just a type. "<code>customer_id</code>: the internal numeric ID, not the email address; obtain it from <code>lookup_customer</code> if you only have an email" prevents an entire class of error that no type annotation catches.<br><br><strong>Handle the resolution problem.</strong> A common failure is the model inventing an ID it does not have. The fix is structural: provide a lookup tool and make the description state that IDs must be obtained, never guessed. Then validate — reject IDs that do not exist with an instructive error.<br><br><strong>Normalize in the tool layer</strong> rather than demanding perfection from the model: accept several date formats and parse them, do fuzzy matching on enum values, trim and case-normalize. Being liberal in what you accept converts many failures into successes at near-zero cost.<br><br><strong>And return instructive errors</strong> on the rest, so the model self-corrects on the next step — with the specific field, the received value, and the expected format.` },

{ q: 'How do computer-use agents work?', tags: [], a: `Computer-use agents operate a GUI the way a person does: take a screenshot, reason about what is on screen, and emit low-level actions — click at coordinates, type text, scroll, key press — then take another screenshot and repeat. The model is multimodal, and the action space is mouse and keyboard rather than APIs.<br><br><strong>Why this exists:</strong> most software has no API. Legacy internal systems, vendor portals, and desktop applications are only reachable through their interface, so GUI control is the universal fallback integration.<br><br><strong>Why it is hard:</strong> grounding — mapping "click the Submit button" to precise pixel coordinates — is the core difficulty and the main source of failure. Screens change, elements move, modals appear, pages load asynchronously. Each step consumes a full screenshot, which is thousands of tokens, so trajectories are expensive and long. And error recovery is genuinely difficult: a misclick can land the agent in an unexpected state with no clean way to detect or undo it.<br><br><strong>Practical mitigations:</strong> prefer accessibility trees or DOM structure over raw pixels where available (far more reliable grounding than coordinates); use APIs whenever one exists — computer use should be the last resort, not the default; verify state after each action rather than assuming success; and run in a VM so the blast radius is contained.<br><br><strong>The safety point:</strong> a computer-use agent has, by construction, the permissions of the user session it drives — including their email, files, and any logged-in application. That is close to maximal privilege, so isolation, credential scoping, and human confirmation for consequential actions are mandatory rather than optional.` },

{ q: 'How does LangChain work?', tags: [], a: `LangChain is a framework providing abstractions over LLM application components: model wrappers with a common interface, prompt templates, output parsers, retrievers and vector store integrations, tools, memory, and composition via LCEL (the pipe-operator expression language) which chains runnables and gives streaming, batching, and async for free.<br><br><strong>What it genuinely buys you:</strong> a large integration surface — dozens of model providers, vector stores, and document loaders behind one interface — so swapping components is cheap. Plus prebuilt implementations of common patterns (RAG chains, agents, conversational memory) that get a prototype running in an afternoon.<br><br><strong>The honest criticisms,</strong> which an interviewer is often probing for: the abstraction layers can obscure what is actually being sent to the model, which makes debugging harder exactly when it matters; the API surface has churned significantly across versions; and for a well-understood pipeline the framework often adds more indirection than value — a direct SDK call plus your own retrieval code is frequently simpler and easier to reason about.<br><br><strong>How I'd use it:</strong> excellent for prototyping and for the integration layer (document loaders and vector store clients are real work you should not rewrite). For the core application logic in production, I'd generally write it directly, because prompt assembly and control flow are the things you most need to see and version.<br><br><strong>The related point:</strong> LangGraph — the same team's graph-based orchestration library — is the more defensible piece for production agents, since explicit state and control flow are exactly what free-form chains lack.` },

{ q: 'How does LangGraph work?', tags: [], a: `LangGraph models an agent as a <strong>state machine</strong>: you define a typed state object, nodes that are functions transforming that state (an LLM call, a tool, arbitrary code), and edges — including conditional edges whose target is computed from the current state. The graph is compiled and executed, with state threaded through explicitly.<br><br><strong>What it fixes relative to free-form agent loops:</strong> control flow becomes explicit and inspectable rather than emergent from model decisions. You can see every reachable path, unit-test individual nodes, and reason about termination. State is a typed object you own, not an implicit accumulation in a message list.<br><br><strong>The features that matter in production:</strong> <em>checkpointing</em> — state is persisted at node boundaries, so long runs resume after failure rather than restarting, and this is also what makes human-in-the-loop work as a genuine interrupt (pause at a node, wait for approval, continue). <em>Cycles</em> with structure, so retry and refinement loops exist but through defined edges with conditions. <em>Parallel</em> fan-out and join for independent branches. And streaming of intermediate state for progress display.<br><br><strong>The tradeoff:</strong> you trade autonomy for control. A graph constrains what the agent can do — which is precisely the point for reliability, and precisely the limitation when a task genuinely requires open-ended exploration.<br><br><strong>The design guidance:</strong> put the model where judgment is genuinely needed and make the rest deterministic. Most production agents are better as graphs with a few model-decided branches than as a fully model-driven loop, because that is what makes them testable and cost-bounded.` },

{ q: 'What is OKF (Open Knowledge Format)?', tags: [], a: `I don't have reliable information about a widely-adopted standard by this exact name in the AI engineering context, and I'd rather say that than invent a specification. The acronym collides with several unrelated things — Open Knowledge Foundation, and various proposed knowledge-interchange formats — so the honest answer in an interview is to ask what the interviewer is referring to.<br><br><strong>What is worth knowing is the problem space it names:</strong> portable formats for representing structured knowledge that agents and LLM systems consume. The established options there are RDF/OWL and JSON-LD for semantic graphs, property-graph formats used by Neo4j and similar, and schema.org vocabularies for typed entities. In agent contexts, MCP has become the standard for exposing tools and resources rather than for representing knowledge itself.<br><br><strong>The engineering point that generalizes:</strong> knowledge-format standards matter for the same reason MCP does — avoiding M×N bespoke integrations between knowledge sources and consumers. But they consistently under-deliver relative to their promise, because the hard part is not serialization; it is ontology agreement and entity resolution across sources, which no format solves.<br><br><strong>How I'd handle this in a real interview:</strong> say plainly that I don't know that specific term, ask for context, and then reason about it from the adjacent standards I do know. Fabricating a confident description of a specification is exactly the failure mode we spend our time engineering LLM systems to avoid.` },
]};
